In TypeScript, duck typing is often implemented using interfaces. An interface defines the structure of an object, including its properties and methods. When a variable is declared with an interface type, the TypeScript compiler checks that the object assigned to that variable has all the required properties and methods defined in the interface. This check is done at compile-time, which allows the TypeScript compiler to catch errors early and provide better tooling support, such as auto-completion and refactoring.

